[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r1,r11,r12
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: IPv4 Neighbor/ARP reachability (NUD) on Linux L3 interfaces.
- Mechanism: All intermediate routers are aging their ARP/neighbor entries every 30 seconds (Linux default base_reachable_time_ms). When an entry goes STALE, the next packet triggers an ARP probe; during that probe initial packets are queued/dropped, briefly stalling the flow. Because your path crosses several L3 hops (r1 → r11 → r12), these 30 s refreshes sometimes align, compounding into a noticeable “freeze” that then clears—giving the impression the route is changing even though it is not.
- Localization and evidence:
  - The path is stable: traceroute from pc0 always shows 20.1.1.1 → 111.1.1.2 → 112.1.1.2 → 120.1.1.10; r1 consistently routes 120.0.0.0/8 via 111.1.1.2. OSPF/EIGRP neighbors are steady (no flaps) on r1/r11/r12.
  - ARP churn observed on the implicated links: ip neigh shows STALE entries with probes on each hop (e.g., r1 for 111.1.1.2, r11 for 112.1.1.2, r12 for 120.1.1.10). All three routers report net.ipv4.neigh.<if>.base_reachable_time_ms = 30000, confirming the 30 s refresh cycle.
- Root cause: Default, aggressive 30 s ARP/NUD reachability timers on r1/r11/r12 cause periodic neighbor probing across multiple hops, producing synchronous micro-outages under load.
- Corrective fix: Increase neighbor reachability timers on transit interfaces (e.g., sysctl -w net.ipv4.neigh.eth1.base_reachable_time_ms=300000 on r1; similarly on r11 eth0/eth1 and r12 eth1). Persist in /etc/sysctl.conf. Optionally add static ARP for host default gateways or use /30 point-to-point masks to limit ARP domains. This will eliminate the periodic “pulse” without changing routing.
=== END ===